author ai
group gui
note Back and forward, the way a browser does it. Nothing in the app calls a 'navigate' function: at the end of every frame, after the action queue has been applied and the views have reported their scroll offsets, the current place is compared with the place at the end of the last frame, and if the reader moved the old one is pushed onto the history. So any click anywhere - a symbol in a list, an xref, a step, a command in the Console - becomes history for free, and no feature has to remember to record itself. A place is the tour by name, the step by StepId, the focused symbol as a SymbolKey and the file by path, so the history survives a re-index, a reload of the map and steps moving. Beside it a place keeps its Viewport - the centre tab, the line selection, the document's and the Source view's scroll and the graph camera - The centre tab is part of what is compared, so switching tabs is its own place and back undoes that switch. Scroll offsets and the graph camera are restored and are not compared, so scrolling updates the place being left. A jump (go-to-definition, go-to-line) that changes only the line selection is a move. Each arrow-key walk is its own place, so back undoes one step of the walk. All of it lives in Nav, which only nav.rs changes.

